\DOC LOCAL_TOMONT_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_TOMONT_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges a called subroutine in an {eventually} goal by applying its proved
specification, advancing the symbolic state to a fresh successor state at the
return point.

\DESCRIBE
The single {int} argument is the index of the current symbolic state, i.e. the
state variable that the goal's {eventually} conclusion is stated about; the
state produced is the one at the next index.
{
   A ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s' /\ C s0 s') s_n
   ================================================  LOCAL_TOMONT_TAC n
   A' ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s' /\ C s0 s') s_m
}
\noindent The postcondition is unchanged and {s_m} is a new, later state
variable. In the new assumption list {A'}, the persistent state-independent
assumptions of {A} (bounds, nonoverlapping and arithmetic facts about the data)
are kept unchanged. All assumptions mentioning the old state — register, flag
and memory reads, the {bytes_loaded} fact, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
frame relating the initial state to the old state — are removed, and replaced
by corresponding assumptions about the new state: the program-image fact; the
reads preserved across the call, with the program counter and stack pointer set
to the return site and restored frame; a new assumption describing the
subroutine's result in the destination memory region; and an extended
{MAYCHANGE} frame from the initial state to the new state, including the
components the call may have clobbered. Flag reads and scratch-register reads
of the old state are simply dropped.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour, on
goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement with the full assumption
context of a machine-code proof.

\ENDDOC
